# ODONTOGRAM PRE-CONSENT AUTHORING FOUNDATION

REVISION-RME-CONSENT-ODONTOGRAM-PRECONSENT-EDIT-1 — durable rules.

This rule is **authoritative** over
`109-rme-exam-consent-odontogram-history.mdc` wherever the two disagree. It
withdraws exactly ONE clause of CORRECTIVE-03 — that a signed consent is a
precondition of authoring the ACTIVE odontogram. Everything else CORRECTIVE-03
established remains in force, including the parts that make this change safe.

## 1. The rule

After a Doctor explicitly starts the examination, the authorized Doctor may
author and save the active visit's Native Odontogram **even when the same-visit
Consent Form has not yet been signed**.

Consent remains mandatory before the Doctor may author or modify the active RME.

Consent remains mandatory before the Doctor may finish the examination.

Pre-consent Native Odontogram authoring does **not** bypass examination-start
state, Doctor authorization, visit ownership, patient scope, historical Native
immutability, Legacy immutability, branch scope, or any other clinical
authorization.

Saving an Odontogram **never** implicitly signs Consent and **never** implicitly
finishes the examination.

Odontogram data saved before Consent **remains intact** after Consent is
subsequently signed.

## 2. The matrix

| state | Odontogram | RME | Selesai Pemeriksaan |
|---|---|---|---|
| `registered` / `waiting` | **DENY** | DENY | DENY |
| `in_progress`, consent UNSIGNED | **ALLOW** | DENY | DENY |
| `in_progress`, consent VALID | ALLOW | ALLOW | ALLOW (other guards apply) |
| `cashier_pending` | DENY | DENY | n/a |
| `completed` / `cancelled` | DENY | DENY | n/a |
| historical native chart (any state) | DENY | DENY | n/a |
| published Legacy Odontogram | DENY | n/a | n/a |

The `RME = DENY` cells above are about the SERVER. What the page may OFFER in
those same states — in particular the "Buat Halaman RM Pertama" control on a
patient with no sheet yet — is owned by
`135-rme-authoring-ui-capability-mirror.mdc`, which extends this matrix with
that column. The two must stay consistent: this rule denies the write, and that
rule denies the offer.

## 3. Why the odontogram, and only the odontogram

Charting teeth is **observation**. The doctor looks in the mouth and records what
is already there, and that observation is precisely what the patient is then
asked to consent to. A `PERSETUJUAN TINDAKAN MEDIS` names the treatment, so
demanding the signature first made the workflow **circular**: the form could not
be completed until the finding existed, and the finding could not be recorded
until the form was signed.

The RME note and the finish transition are different acts and stay gated:

- the RME records the treatment **DECISION**, not the observation;
- finishing hands the visit to the cashier **as a bill**.

Do not generalise this rule into "clinical authoring no longer needs consent".
It applies to `trx_odontograms` on the live encounter and to nothing else.

## 4. Where it is enforced

**One authority, one method.** `RmeVisitConsentService::assertOdontogramAuthoringAllowed()`
is the single place the odontogram rule lives. It checks, in order, so the
refusal names the real reason:

1. the patient HAS a single current `in_progress` encounter — ambiguity (two
   `in_progress` visits) fails closed, exactly as for the RME gate;
2. the actor may work on that encounter (`RmeWorkingBranchScope` +
   `DoctorPatientScopeService`); an unresolvable actor fails closed;
3. the chart being written IS that encounter's own chart.

There is deliberately **no fourth condition**. Its absence is documented in the
method, not merely implied, so nothing there reads as an oversight.

All three write paths funnel through it — `OdontogramService::saveForVisit()`,
`::updatePlaceholder()`, `::finalize()` — and each asserts **before** the insert,
so a refused encounter leaves no trace.

- `OdontogramPolicy` is unchanged and still answers only the
  role/branch/patient-scope question; the refusal carries a clear reason rather
  than a bare 403.
- `getOrCreateForVisit()` still has **zero production callers**, still asserts
  nothing, and must keep having none — pinned by
  `RepositoryArtifactHygieneTest`.

**Do not add a second consent check anywhere.** Not in the controller, not in a
FormRequest, not in Blade, not in JavaScript. If the odontogram rule ever needs
to change again, it changes in that one method.

## 5. Odontogram finalization is not examination completion

`OdontogramService::finalize()` is a **chart-level** lifecycle act. Since Sprint
59 a finalized chart is still revisable, and finalizing one leaves the visit
`in_progress`. It routes through the same single assertion as every other
odontogram write, and is therefore also permitted pre-consent. Splitting it out
would create a second, divergent consent rule for no clinical gain.

"Selesai Pemeriksaan" (`in_progress -> cashier_pending`) is the **workflow**
transition, is a different fact, and stays consent-gated in
`ClinicVisitService::transitionStatus()`.

## 6. The page must agree with the server

`canAuthorOdontogramFor()` is the presentation predicate and delegates to the
same assertion, so the controls the page renders are the ones the server will
accept. Never derive editability in Blade.

The odontogram page shows a **pending-consent reminder, not a refusal**, while
the signature is outstanding: charting is available, and the RME and "Selesai
Pemeriksaan" are not. This is not decoration — the doctor can now complete the
whole chart without ever meeting the gate, and would otherwise first learn the
visit is stuck when they try to finish it.

Any wording that tells a user consent is required before the odontogram may be
edited is **stale** and must be corrected wherever it is found.

The RME side of this same requirement was NOT enforced when this rule was
written, and the gap reached production: the RM workspace empty state offered an
actionable "Buat Halaman RM Pertama" while the server refused the create. See
`135-rme-authoring-ui-capability-mirror.mdc`, which closes it and states the
general form — a permission check is not a capability check.

## 7. Testing

- Prove the rule **server-side**: assert the service/HTTP outcome, never the
  presence or absence of a button.
- `tests/Feature/RME/RmeConsentOdontogramPreConsentEditTest.php` is the pinned
  suite and is declared in `config/ci_runner.php` `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`,
  so a rename must move the coverage and can never silently drop it.
- Tests that are not about consent may seed one with `rmeSignedConsentFor()`.
  Tests that ARE about consent must go through `RmeVisitConsentService`.
- The regression guards matter as much as the new permission. Any change here
  must keep pinning: RME still blocked pre-consent, finish still blocked
  pre-consent (including when the chart is complete), charting before
  "Mulai Pemeriksaan" still refused, non-Doctor and other-Doctor still refused,
  historical native and published Legacy still immutable, no consent or status
  side effect from a save, and pre-consent charting surviving the signature.
